<code>assert \old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) && !\old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) - 1;</code>

However, the above assertion is incorrect because it doesn't properly capture the condition under which the number of sets decreases. The correct assertion should be:

<code>assert !\old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) - 1;</code>

This ensures that the number of sets decreases by 1 only if the two elements were not initially in the same set.
--------------------
<code>assert \old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) : assert !\old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) - 1;</code>

However, based on the given rules and the structure of the comment, we need to simplify it to a single assert statement using the `=>` syntax:

<code>assert !\old(this.inSameSet(element1, element2)) => this.numberOfSets() == \old(this.numberOfSets()) - 1;</code>

This captures the essence that if the two elements were not in the same set initially, the number of sets should decrease by 1 after the union operation.
